# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aristotle-ethics-2.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1 and aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1.3 have "transferred → exceeds_twice" and "only_taken → exceeds_once" and "transferred → greater_exceeds_mean ∧ mean_exceeds_less"; aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1.2 has "transferred → exceeds_twice" and "only_taken → exceeds_once" and "transferred → greater_exceeds_mean ∧ mean_exceeds_less" and "transferred"; they differ when only "the latter exceeds the former by twice that portion", "the portion had been merely taken from the former and not added to the latter" and "the latter would have exceeded the former only by that one portion" are true, which the first allows
aristotle-ethics-2.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered yes, which keeps aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1 and aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the latter exceeds the former by twice that portion", "the portion had been merely taken from the former and not added to the latter" and "the latter would have exceeded the former only by that one portion" are true and "from one of two equal magnitudes somewhat has been taken and added to the other", "the greater exceeds the mean by one portion" and "the mean exceeds by one portion that magnitude from which the portion was taken" are false. Is S2, "For when from one of two equal magnitudes somewhat has been taken and added to the other, this latter exceeds the former by twice that portion: if it had been merely taken from the former and not added to the latter, then the latter would have exceeded the former only by that one portion; but in the other case, the greater exceeds the mean by one, and the mean exceeds also by one that magnitude from which the portion was taken.", true then?
aristotle-ethics-2.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1 has "greater_exceeds_mean ∧ mean_exceeds_less → O(add_to_less ∧ take_from_greater)"; aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1.2 and aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "the greater exceeds the mean by one portion" and "the mean exceeds by one portion that magnitude from which the portion was taken" are true, which the second allows
aristotle-ethics-2.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S3 is read aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1.2 and aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1.3 with no modality; aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1 as what ought to be or is permitted: the text does not say which it means
aristotle-ethics-2.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1, to: Suppose "the greater exceeds the mean by one portion" and "the mean exceeds by one portion that magnitude from which the portion was taken" are true and "it ought to be that (the excess of the mean over the less is added to the less and the excess of the greater over the mean is taken from the greater)" is false. Is S3, "By this illustration, then, we obtain a rule to determine what one ought to take from him who has the greater, and what to add to him who has the less.", true then?
aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1 has "transferred"; aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1.2 has "greater_exceeds_mean ∧ mean_exceeds_less → O add_to_less ∧ O take_from_greater"; they differ when only "from one of two equal magnitudes somewhat has been taken and added to the other", "the greater exceeds the mean by one portion", "the mean exceeds by one portion that magnitude from which the portion was taken" and "it ought to be that the excess of the greater over the mean is taken from the greater" are true, which the first allows; aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1.3 has nothing from it
aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1 and aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1.2 take for granted "from one of two equal magnitudes somewhat has been taken and added to the other"; aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1.3 does not
aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered no, which keeps aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "from one of two equal magnitudes somewhat has been taken and added to the other"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: U2: aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1 and aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1.2 take for granted "if (the greater exceeds the mean by one portion and the mean exceeds by one portion that magnitude from which the portion was taken), then (it ought to be that the excess of the mean over the less is added to the less and it ought to be that the excess of the greater over the mean is taken from the greater)"; aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1.3 does not
aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1 and aristotle-ethics-2.logic.r1.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (the greater exceeds the mean by one portion and the mean exceeds by one portion that magnitude from which the portion was taken), then (it ought to be that the excess of the mean over the less is added to the less and it ought to be that the excess of the greater over the mean is taken from the greater)"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
